ream calcium level a reflection of balance between release and uptake, reinforcing why it is clinically meaningful within a metabolic panel context. [Data: Relationships (10964, 11716, 11717)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Hypercalcemia is a named abnormal-condition pathway the CMP is used to detect\",\"explanation\":\"The dataset explicitly connects CMP to hypercalcemia, stating that CMP “looks for higher-than-normal calcium levels called hypercalcemia.” This establishes an interpretive “alert condition” pathway: abnormal CMP calcium values can indicate clinically important states requiring further assessment depending on patient context. [Data: Entities (5224, 4226); Relationships (11559)]\\n\\nThe calcium entity description further frames hypercalcemia as an example of an abnormal CMP calcium value that is clinically important and may prompt additional assessment. In the community structure, hypercalcemia thus functions as a key outcome/flag arising from CMP measurement of calcium rather than as an independent measurement. [Data: Entities (4226, 5224); Relationships (11559)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Bone remodeling provides the mechanistic context for calcium changes relevant to CMP interpretation\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes a mechanistic subnetwork describing bone remodeling as a balance between bone breakdown and bone rebuilding. Bone breakdown is described as the process that releases calcium into the bloodstream, and bone rebuilding is described as formation using calcium from the bloodstream. This provides a plausible physiologic context for why bloodstream calcium can rise or fall and why monitoring it in a CMP can be clinically relevant. [Data: Entities (5310, 5311); Relationships (11716, 11717, 10964)]\\n\\nThe calcium description emphasizes that most calcium is stored in bone and is continuously regulated through remodeling (release during breakdown, uptake during formation). This narrative ties the calcium measurement (CMP analyte) to physiologic processes,